          "text": "{\"relation\":\"DIVERGENT\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The recognizable consensus identifies oil primarily with the Holy Spirit’s anointing, empowerment, and consecrating presence, while also allowing secondary associations such as healing, joy, readiness, light-bearing witness, and good works.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book explicitly rejects the Holy Spirit identification and defines oil as the pressed and tested fruit of proven works that produces visible light.\",\"rationale\":\"Although the blind responses recognize minority readings involving readiness, sustained witness, and sometimes good works, the book elevates that secondary interpretive strand into oil’s central meaning and expressly denies the dominant Spirit interpretation. It therefore displaces, rather than merely refines, the consensus.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Exodus 27:20 establishes that beaten olive oil fuels light, and Matthew 5:16 associates visible light with good works, but Matthew 5 does not mention oil and Matthew 25 never identifies its oil as works. The conclusion depends on linking distinct passages and treating physical features such as pressing as symbolic, rather than on an explicit scriptural equation of oil with proven works.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
